+# Specification: managed Arrow first-insert index
+
+Date: 2026-11-13. Status: candidate, acceptance pending.
+See [intent](intent.md), [plan](plan.md) and [evidence](validation.md).
+
+## Contract and boundaries
+
+After converting and checking the first nonempty Arrow slice, an eligible
+ordinary managed writer creates its already registered private output index
+before inserting rows. Existing top-level global CREATE admission gates also
+require the exact registered output directory to be empty before and after
+CREATE. The prefix is newly allocated and exclusively owned; row insertion
+preflights both HASH absence and directory-member absence. Each row is one
+complete HASH image, written by the existing bounded atomic row-batch script.
+Empty input retains the deferred index path. Runtime-only eligibility excludes
+Flex, retained operations and imported/provenance-bearing outputs.
+
+Late TAG/NaN value facts affect query planning metadata, not the physical Search
+schema. Seal compares the exact index-creation arguments and identity. Any
+legacy physical mutation, import or replace-mode row batch invalidates the
+insert-only proof; seal creates/populates a fresh final index and retires the
+early one. Ordinary SQL remains deferred. Private reads still use authoritative
+directories; early index presence is not a visibility or readiness assertion.
+
+Every publication retains lease/source validation, final FT.INFO identity,
+prefix, indexing-error/readiness and exact directory/cardinality checks, plus
+the warning-free native indexed-row-ID proof. Only a typed query ACL refusal
+may select the existing private initial-scan fallback. Unknown outcomes,
+transport failures or warnings cannot select fallback or cause mutation replay.
+
+No stored fields or storage-protocol version changes. Every index retains the
+registered data ID in its unique name. Existing data retirement drops all such
+indexes; existing orphan-index cleanup handles CREATE arriving after retirement.
+An uncertain CREATE/DROP aborts, and unknown row replies use the existing
+read-only outcome resolution without replaying input or mutations.
+
+## Acceptance
+
+Prove first-row timing, complete typed/null images across bounded batches,
+zero-row behavior, invalidation/rebuild, cancellation/reader failure and
+unknown-reply behavior. Preserve native value/full-fetch validation and ACL
+fallback tests. Accept the optimization only after a comparable native rerun
+and diagnostic attribution show supported gain; retain all attempts and label
+any failed/negative experiment.
